---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/ui/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/ui/Footer.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How It Works — BPM & Key Detection | FindSongKey"
  description="Learn how FindSongKey detects BPM and musical key from your audio files using advanced signal processing, entirely in your browser."
  canonical="https://findsongkeybpm.com/how-it-works"
>
  <Header />
  <main class="content-page" id="how-it-works">
    <h1>How it works</h1>

    <h2>1. Upload your audio</h2>
    <p>
      Drag and drop an audio file onto the analyzer, or click to browse your files.
      We support MP3, WAV, FLAC, and M4A formats up to 20MB. Your file never leaves
      your device — everything runs locally in your browser.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Audio decoding</h2>
    <p>
      Your browser's built-in Web Audio API decodes the audio file into raw waveform
      data. If the file is stereo, we downmix it to mono. If the sample rate differs
      from 44,100 Hz, we resample it for optimal analysis accuracy.
    </p>

    <h2>3. BPM detection</h2>
    <p>
      We use the RhythmExtractor2013 algorithm from the Essentia library, which combines
      multiple tempo estimation methods including onset detection, beat tracking, and
      autocorrelation. This multi-feature approach provides robust tempo estimation
      across a wide range of musical genres.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Onset detection identifies the start of each musical event</li>
      <li>Autocorrelation finds repeating rhythmic patterns</li>
      <li>Beat tracking aligns detected onsets to a tempo grid</li>
      <li>The final BPM is the consensus of multiple estimators</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>4. Key detection</h2>
    <p>
      Musical key is detected using Essentia's KeyExtractor algorithm. It analyzes the
      harmonic content of the audio by computing a chromagram (pitch class profile) and
      matching it against key profiles using the Krumhansl-Schmuckler algorithm.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The audio is segmented into overlapping frames</li>
      <li>A chromagram extracts pitch class energy for each frame</li>
      <li>Key profiles for all 24 major and minor keys are compared</li>
      <li>The best-matching key is returned with a confidence score</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>5. Camelot Wheel</h2>
    <p>
      Once the key is detected, we map it to its Camelot Wheel code. The Camelot
      system assigns each of the 24 musical keys a number (1–12) and a letter (A for
      minor, B for major). DJs use this system to find harmonically compatible tracks
      for smooth mixing transitions.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Same number, switch letter → relative major/minor (always compatible)</li>
      <li>Same letter, ±1 number → adjacent keys (smooth transition)</li>
      <li>Moving up in numbers → gradually increases energy</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Privacy first</h2>
    <p>
      All analysis happens entirely in your browser using WebAssembly (WASM). Your
      audio files are never uploaded to any server. They stay on your device from
      start to finish. We don't track what you analyze, and we don't store any results.
    </p>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
